WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.400
Let’s talk about US sanctions on Iran

00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:04.920
because the US president 
keeps piling them on.

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:08.040
Powerful sanctions will 
go into full effect.

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:09.800
but Iran isn’t bowing.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:16.960
Now, according to this online tracker

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:19.840
America has more 
sanctions against Iran

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:22.040
than it does on North Korea, Cuba

00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:24.480
Venezuela and Libya combined.

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:26.720
So what exactly are 
those sanctions

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:28.800
and more importantly, 
are they working?

00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:36.640
The big picture is this:

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:39.400
the US and Iran are, 
in a way, at war.

00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:42.880
States like these and 
their terrorist allies

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:45.360
constitute an axis of evil. 

00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:49.360
But they don’t fight
 face to face

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:51.520
Instead, they antagonise
 each other.

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:54.240
Iran gets involved in conflicts 
around the Middle East 

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:57.840
by backing armed groups 
and US rivals like Russia.

00:00:57.840 --> 00:00:59.640
And the US's main weapon?

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:00.480
Sanctions.

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:02.680
Now the simple definition 
of a sanction is:

00:01:02.680 --> 00:01:04.960
“an official order, taken 
against a country 

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:07.640
in order to make it obey
international law.”

00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:11.240
That could include bans on travel, transactions or trade.

00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:13.680
And in Iran’s case, 
it’s all of those.

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:18.080
Any kind of exchange 
that directly or indirectly 

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:22.400
might benefit the Iranian 
government or any agency of it

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:26.000
or any individual who may 
directly or indirectly be

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:29.920
affiliated with it is prohibited 
under American law.

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:32.880
Now we’ve read the US Treasury’s blacklist for Iran 

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:34.240
and it is long.

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:37.000
The big ones are: a ban on Iran’s biggest industry

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:37.920
which is oil

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:41.920
and a ban on any kind of financial transactions with Iran.

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:44.920
Plus its entire steel, mining, 
coal sectors 

00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:48.760
dozens of individual people and ministries in Iran’s government

00:01:48.760 --> 00:01:50.800
banks, insurance, 
construction companies

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:52.560
ships and aircraft.

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:55.040
There’s even a ban 
on Persian carpets

00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:57.080
one of Iran’s 
biggest exports

00:01:57.080 --> 00:02:00.240
and its pistachio industry.

00:02:01.880 --> 00:02:05.440
Now, the penalties for violating those sanctions are serious.

00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:09.880
The US government can fine a person or company up to $1 million

00:02:09.880 --> 00:02:12.840
or hand down a 20 year
jail sentence.

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:15.120
Even if they’re not American.

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:18.160
Because the US has 
such a strong dollar

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:21.680
and so many transactions are conducted in dollars

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:23.880
it means that US sanctions

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:27.480
would affect any EU company doing business with Iran.

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:30.120
Between having sanctions on
 its main industries

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:32.960
and being blocked from trading 
with other countries

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:37.760
Iran has become one of the most isolated economies in the world.

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:40.560
So when did this sanctions 
war start?

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:41.960
More than 40 years ago.

00:02:45.800 --> 00:02:48.800
The US long supported Iran’s 
monarch the Shah

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:52.280
but in 1979, the Islamic 
Revolution swept

00:02:52.280 --> 00:02:55.120
cleric Ayatollah Khomeini 
to power.

00:02:55.120 --> 00:02:58.440
A group of Iranian revolutionaries stormed the US embassy

00:02:58.440 --> 00:03:00.840
and imprisoned its staff 
for over a year.

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:03.800
That led to the very first round 
of sanctions on Iran

00:03:03.800 --> 00:03:07.720
with the US freezing billions of dollars in Iranian government assets.

00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:09.360
and it was just the beginning.

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:12.600
Over the next several decades, 
Iran came under more 

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:16.000
international sanctions over  
human rights abuses.

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:18.960
Iran was also sanctioned for 
sending billions of dollars 

00:03:18.960 --> 00:03:20.600
to armed groups in the Middle East

00:03:20.600 --> 00:03:24.000
like Hezbollah in Lebanon, or 
Hamas in Palestine.

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:27.120
But it was Iran’s decision to 
develop nuclear power

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:29.280
that really got the US worried

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:32.880
because Iran now had the tools 
to build a nuclear bomb.

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:39.440
Since 2003, a nuclear crisis has been brewing between Iran

00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:42.120
the United States and 
its European allies 

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:46.280
when it was revealed that 
Iran has two nuclear

00:03:46.280 --> 00:03:50.480
facilities that it did 
not declare to the IAEA.

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:54.280
So in 2011, the US together 
with the UN issued

00:03:54.280 --> 00:03:57.560
a massive heap of economic 
sanctions on Iran

00:03:57.560 --> 00:04:01.360
to (a) stop any money flowing to 
its nuclear programme

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:06.200
and (b) to pressure Iran into potentially striking a diplomatic deal.

00:04:06.200 --> 00:04:07.760
And that part worked.

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:12.280
In 2015, after long and tough negotiations, a deal known as

00:04:12.280 --> 00:04:15.880
the Joint Comprehensive Plan of Action was signed.

00:04:15.880 --> 00:04:17.280
“A historic deal.”

00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:19.640
“The landmark nuclear agreement 
will mean that Iran

00:04:19.640 --> 00:04:22.200
has to sharply curtail its 
nuclear programme.”

00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:27.160
“The Iranian foreign minister receives a hero’s welcome back in Tehran.

00:04:27.160 --> 00:04:30.560
International sanctions against Iran 
will now be lifted.” 

00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:34.680
We've achieved this historic 
progress through diplomacy 

00:04:34.680 --> 00:04:37.240
without resorting to another war 
in the Middle East.

00:04:37.240 --> 00:04:39.720
But the truth is that many 
US lawmakers

00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:41.760
Republicans in particular

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:44.960
didn’t trust Iran would keep 
its end of the deal.

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:47.600
Neither did one of America’s 
closest allies.

00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:52.560
Iran's rulers promised to 
destroy my country.

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:55.920
Murder my people.

00:04:55.920 --> 00:05:00.000
Perhaps you can now
 understand why Israel  

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:02.400
is not joining you in 
celebrating this deal.

00:05:05.840 --> 00:05:09.800
So as soon as Donald Trump became president, he backed out.

00:05:09.800 --> 00:05:13.440
I am announcing today that 
the United States 

00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:18.520
will withdraw from the Iran 
nuclear deal.

00:05:18.520 --> 00:05:20.760
All the old sanctions came back.

00:05:20.760 --> 00:05:23.760
And more than 700 new ones 
were issued against  

00:05:23.760 --> 00:05:26.080
Iranian individuals and companies.

00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:27.520
It’s what the Trump administration 

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:30.000
calls its “maximum 
pressure campaign."

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:31.320
So when people ask: 

00:05:31.320 --> 00:05:34.600
has the US sanctions programme 
worked on Iran? 

00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:36.920
Well, that’s debatable. 

00:05:36.920 --> 00:05:41.000
These new sanctions have nearly killed the entire Iran nuclear deal.

00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:43.000
And in response, the
 Iranians have said:

00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:45.560
Well, what’s the point of us 
holding up our end?

00:05:45.560 --> 00:05:49.160
So they’ve now restarted some low-level nuclear activity.

00:05:49.160 --> 00:05:54.240
And they will probably continue to do so until the EU takes action

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:57.280
to deliver on some of their commitments 

00:05:57.280 --> 00:06:00.080
which they are not able to deliver because of US pressure. 

00:06:00.080 --> 00:06:03.240
The eventual outcome could be
an Iran withdrawal  

00:06:03.240 --> 00:06:08.120
from the nuclear accord and also the death of the whole deal.

00:06:08.120 --> 00:06:11.440
Sanctions have managed to
 cripple Iran’s economy.

00:06:11.440 --> 00:06:13.920
But Iranians are getting
 around them.

00:06:16.200 --> 00:06:17.880
“Human mules have 
carried smuggled 

00:06:17.880 --> 00:06:20.040
goods across the 
border for years.

00:06:20.040 --> 00:06:22.800
Electronics, phones, 
beauty products

00:06:22.800 --> 00:06:25.680
medicines, cigarettes, 
the list goes on.”

00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:31.120
Long years of sanctions have led the Iranians to develop many

00:06:31.120 --> 00:06:37.240
of their own domestic products inside that have substituted

00:06:37.240 --> 00:06:38.880
for those things that they 
can’t bring in.

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:41.120
When sanctions come,
there’s a dip. 

00:06:41.120 --> 00:06:44.880
And then, somehow, the 
economy starts reacting 

00:06:44.880 --> 00:06:47.560
and responding 
and compensating 

00:06:47.560 --> 00:06:51.040
and within three or four years,
it’s back to growth.

00:06:51.040 --> 00:06:53.480
Another question is whether 
Iran’s government

00:06:53.480 --> 00:06:56.480
can survive many more
years of instability.

00:06:56.480 --> 00:06:59.280
Don’t get me wrong, it still has
 a lot of support.

00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:01.920
But recent protests across
 Iranian cities have 

00:07:01.920 --> 00:07:04.720
exposed some discontent with 
those in power.

00:07:07.160 --> 00:07:10.640
“Anger, frustration and growing concern in Tehran.”

00:07:10.640 --> 00:07:13.040
The protests erupted after 
Iran’s government

00:07:13.040 --> 00:07:14.760
which has been strapped
 for cash

00:07:14.760 --> 00:07:17.480
increased the price of fuel
to raise funds.

00:07:21.520 --> 00:07:23.880
In November, thousands of 
people were arrested

00:07:23.880 --> 00:07:26.720
rights groups say more 
than 300 were killed

00:07:26.720 --> 00:07:30.200
and at one point, there was a
 total internet shutdown.

00:07:30.200 --> 00:07:34.000
Iran’s leaders say they were 
responding to a bigger threat.

00:07:53.400 --> 00:07:57.800
The bottom line is: it’s not clear whether the US’s huge web of sanctions 

00:07:57.800 --> 00:08:00.560
is going to lead to any kind of breakthrough with Iran.

00:08:00.560 --> 00:08:03.600
And there’s a whole debate about whether those pressure tactics

00:08:03.600 --> 00:08:08.040
have ever forced any big changes in places like Cuba or North Korea 

00:08:08.040 --> 00:08:11.080
where sanctions have been 
around for decades.

00:08:11.080 --> 00:08:15.080
Those who defend sanctions say the alternative is an actual war.

00:08:15.080 --> 00:08:20.000
But neither the US nor Iran, nor anybody else seem to want that.

